WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.320
Reconnaissance and mapping in an authorized assessment should be organized and controlled.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:13.860
The assessor may collect routes, pages, parameters, forms, workflows, API endpoints, user roles, and observed behaviors.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:20.560
AI can help transform that material into a structured map, but the activity should remain within approved targets and methods.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:23.980
The objective is clarity, not uncontrolled automation.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:38.440
A useful AI workflow is to provide authorized notes and ask for organization: group endpoints by function, list forms that handle sensitive data, identify workflows that need manual review, or produce a checklist aligned to the scope.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:44.260
This can reduce the cognitive load of large applications while keeping the analyst focused on areas that matter.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:47.920
AI can also help compare discovered material against the scope.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:57.020
If a route appears to belong to an excluded system, or if an API references a business area outside the engagement, the assistant can flag it as a question for review.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:02.200
The analyst should treat that as a prompt to check the rules of engagement, not as permission to proceed.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:04.980
Avoid intrusive or out-of-scope automation.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:13.760
AI-generated lists can be helpful, but they should not become a launchpad for uncontrolled requests, high-volume scanning, or testing outside agreed boundaries.

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:18.300
Good mapping supports planning, prioritization, and documentation.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:21.800
It should make the assessment more deliberate, not more reckless.
